  "full_response": "SQL (Structured Query Language) databases and NoSQL databases are two different types of databases used for storing and managing data, each with its own set of features and use cases. Here are the key differences between them:\n\n1. model: A SQL database uses a structured data model with rigidly defined schemas defined using tables with rows and columns. Every table should have a primary key, secondary keys, and defined relationships between the tables. On the other hand, a NoSQL database uses non-structured data models with flexible schemas allowing users to define data types to store based on specific use cases. There are multiple types of NoSQL: document, column, graph, key-value stores.\n\n2. Scalability: A SQL database was originally designed to be horizontal (scaling obsolete; multi-processor) and it can thus not keep pace when the organization expects a source of growth in the number of connections. NoSQL databases, due to their flexible and dynamic schemas, are designed with scalability in mind and can store immense amounts of data. NoSQL databases can be highly scalable, either horizontally or vertically, depending on which type you choose.\n\n3. Data consistency: SQL databases can be either \"strongly consistent,\" meaning data is written immediately and persisted on disk, or synchronized across all nodes for end-to-end consistency. NoSQL databases can also be strongly consistent or \"eventually consistent,\" meaning the data may not be synchronized across nodes but will eventually be.\n\nWhen to use SQL databases:\n\n1. Data integrity is a top priority (although they are not always as strongly consistent as NoSQL databases). Significant relationships between data entities would benefit from using SQL databases.\n\n2. You have defined data and your schema has not changed over time. Technical teams, ensuring that new data fields are accounted for quickly and reliably, would benefit from using SQL databases.\n\n3. You have a predictable and well-defined workload. Companies with predictable workloads such as social media sites that have inherently predictable user action, need to respond with the same type of query.\n\nWhen to use NoSQL databases:\n\n1. The data and schema is dynamic. Tech companies dealing with constantly changing data fields and a rapidly shifting business environment would benefit from using NoSQL databases like MongoDB, CouchDB, etc.\n\n2. You need horizontal scalability to serve more users through many machines while avoiding added complexity in managing distributed deployments. NoSQL databases are and are known for automatically handling distributed systems and can scale horizontally as new nodes appear or disappear, thus improving query time.\n\n3. Duplication of data across machines is",
